          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Why does low iron make people tired?",
          "answer": "Low iron can make you tired because iron is needed to make hemoglobin, the protein in red blood cells that carries oxygen. The iron-deficiency anemia summary explains that when the body runs out of iron stores, red cells are made smaller and paler than normal. Less hemoglobin means less oxygen can be carried around the body, and people often feel this as fatigue, breathlessness on exertion, palpitations, dizziness and cold hands and feet. (Source: *Iron-deficiency anemia*, general clinical reference.)\n\nThe material I searched does not explain the next step, how less oxygen reaches the cells and affects their energy production. I could not find that explanation in the available sources, so I can't describe it reliably here.\n\nFatigue has many causes, and the same reference notes that iron deficiency in an adult who is not pregnant or bleeding heavily raises the question of where the iron was lost. A clinician can check this with a blood count and iron tests. Seek urgent care if tiredness comes with chest pain, breathlessness at rest, fainting, or signs of heavy bleeding such as black or bloody stools.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
